EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-309t4z_connection-control-and-status-ui/README.md:10:1. iOS and macOS users can select an eligible profile, grant required system VPN permission, connect, disconnect, and recover after containing-app relaunch while the Network Extension session remains authoritative. 2. The UI renders exactly the required lifecycle states and never reports connected/full unless the current provider generation confirms TCP, leak-safe DNS, UDP, and required address-family capability. 3. Degraded mode remains connected, names the missing UDP capability and safe limitations, while failed is reserved for a mandatory safe-forwarding failure. 4. Reasserting, disconnecting, stale snapshots, permission denial, configuration failure, host trust/auth failure, and tunnel setup failure have deterministic controls and accessible remediation. 5. QUIC and routing settings expose only policies supported by the accepted M3 contracts, explain compatible versus fail-closed limitations, and apply through versioned profile generations. 6. Deterministic state tests, iOS/macOS UI tests, and physical-device checks verify controls, system VPN indicator, host-app termination continuity, full/degraded transitions, profile switching, accessibility, and screenshots.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-309t4z_connection-control-and-status-ui/README.md:7:In scope: selected-profile controls, connect, disconnect, system VPN permission, owned-manager errors, disconnected, connecting, connected/full, connected/degraded, reasserting, disconnecting, and failed presentation, capability and reason details, profile switching, QUIC Auto/Allow/Block UDP 443 policy, compatible and platform-scoped fail-closed routing choices, stale-state handling, iOS and macOS presentation, accessibility, automated UI tests, and physical system-indicator checks. Out of scope: packet/SSH/relay implementation, redefining capability predicates, release signing, TestFlight, App Review submission, analytics, and legacy public SOCKS controls.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-3tds7d_diagnostics-privacy-and-support/README.md:10:1. Default diagnostics are bounded and limited to approved timestamps, states, lane identifiers, aggregate counts, error domains/codes, memory observations, negotiated algorithms, relay identity, and redacted address-family facts. 2. Automated adversarial tests prove keys, passphrases, packet payloads, DNS names, destination hosts or IPs, full local addresses, shell input, and traffic samples cannot enter logs, snapshots, crash annotations, or support exports. 3. A user can inspect diagnostic categories, preview the exact export manifest, create an export only on explicit action, and delete retained local support data on iOS and macOS. 4. Before first VPN enablement the app explains processed data, the user-controlled SSH exit, exit-host visibility, Relux Works absence from the baseline data path, system-excluded traffic, retention/deletion, and zero traffic telemetry. 5. Extension-to-app diagnostic transfer is versioned, size-bounded, stale-generation safe, and remains useful after containing-app relaunch. 6. Unit, property/fuzz where appropriate, UI, and physical acceptance tests verify redaction, bounds, disclosure gating, export preview/output, deletion, accessibility, and no analytics or traffic-telemetry endpoints.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-n5dt84_onboarding-accessibility-and-migration/README.md:10:1. A first-time user on iOS and macOS can understand the self-hosted SSH model, complete or resume prerequisites, create a profile, trust a host, acknowledge privacy terms, grant VPN permission, connect, understand capability or failure, obtain help, and disconnect. 2. Privacy disclosure appears before the first enable action and onboarding does not claim anonymity, a Relux exit network, or capabilities not present in the selected mode. 3. All M4 surfaces have stable shared accessibility identifiers, meaningful labels/hints/values, logical focus, keyboard operation on macOS, VoiceOver operation, supported text scaling, sufficient contrast, and reduced-motion behavior. 4. User-facing strings are externalized, pseudo-localization and text expansion pass, and the approved launch-locale policy and fallback are documented. 5. A reviewed decision selects legacy SOCKS coexistence, replacement, or retirement and defines handling of host/account/port defaults, existing release history, bundle/storage identity, rollback, and user messaging before migration code runs. 6. Automated UI journeys plus named physical Apple-silicon Mac acceptance, with the iPhone journey deferred with iOS under ADR-024, cover first run, relaunch/resume, permission denial, empty/error recovery, migration path, full/degraded/failure states, diagnostics, accessibility, and screenshot review.
EPIC-260715-21g2pi_product-experience-and-security/STORY-260715-n5dt84_onboarding-accessibility-and-migration/README.md:7:In scope: first-run and resumable onboarding, SSH host prerequisite guidance, privacy acknowledgement before VPN enablement, profile/trust/permission handoffs, empty and recoverable error states, settings/help entry points, VoiceOver and macOS accessibility, keyboard navigation, focus order, Dynamic Type and text scaling, contrast, reduced motion, localization-ready copy, pseudo-localization, shared accessibility identifiers, current SOCKS coexistence/replacement/retirement decision, approved defaults/history migration, iOS/macOS UI tests, and physical end-to-end acceptance. Out of scope: implementing a public SOCKS server, OpenSSH config import, ProxyJump, release notarization, TestFlight upload, App Store submission, regional licensing decisions, and unsupported locale translations.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/README.md:10:Gate A0 is deferred off this epic's critical path under ADR-013 and stays required before App Store distribution and iOS submission, where it must have documented evidence; Gate P0 installs a packet-tunnel provider on the physical Apple-silicon Mac; the physical-iPhone row is a named deferred gap under ADR-024, never inferred from Mac results and never a pass; packet bridge/HEV and one SSH engine pass the M0 matrix; open decisions are recorded with reproducible results.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/README.md:10:1. Evidence confirms Relux Works organization enrollment, team identifier, responsible roles, registered physical devices, and access to manage identifiers, capabilities, certificates, and profiles. 2. A reviewed identity matrix defines unique iOS and macOS host and extension bundle IDs plus the minimum App Group and Keychain groups. 3. Host and extension App IDs and development profiles contain the exact required packet-tunnel, App Group, and Keychain entitlements with no unrelated capability. 4. A disposable provider is installed through its containing app, saves a NETunnelProviderManager configuration, launches, reports a versioned probe result, and stops cleanly on one named Apple-silicon Mac; the physical-iPhone row is a named deferred gap under ADR-024. 5. Exported entitlement and profile inspections match the planned matrix and contain no secret material. 6. A Gate P0 record includes device and OS versions, Xcode and source revisions, bundle/profile identifiers, commands or steps, observed results, failures, and an explicit pass or fail verdict.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/README.md:4:Prove that the Relux Works organization can provision the required host and packet-tunnel extension identities and can install and launch a minimally functional custom VPN provider on the physical Apple-silicon Mac. iOS is deferred under ADR-024; its identifiers stay defined so the iPhone row re-arms unchanged. The result is a reproducible signing and entitlement baseline for later M0 integration, not a production VPN.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-2xnj3v_apple-capability-provisioning/README.md:7:In scope: organization enrollment and required roles; bundle ID, App Group, and Keychain access-group naming; Network Extension packet-tunnel capabilities; development certificates and profiles; disposable iOS and macOS host/provider probes; NETunnelProviderManager configuration; entitlement and embedded-profile inspection; physical-device install, launch, start, and stop evidence. Out of scope: TestFlight or App Store release, Developer ID notarization, production UI, packet forwarding, SSH transport, relay behavior, and storage of certificates, private keys, credentials, or provisioning secrets in the repository or board.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-jnpbyz_packet-bridge-hev-spike/README.md:10:1. The spike uses only NEPacketTunnelFlow and documented Darwin socket APIs and contains no utun control access, descriptor discovery, or private API. 2. Forward and reverse paths preserve packet boundaries, add or validate and strip network-byte-order AF_INET or AF_INET6 headers, batch writes, and count malformed or unsupported frames. 3. EAGAIN, EWOULDBLOCK, and ENOBUFS cause bounded reason-specific drops; EMSGSIZE fails start with attempted and effective limits; persistent errors and cancellation release all tasks and descriptors. 4. Upstream HEV runs with socks5.udp set to tcp and the stated low-memory baseline, and its internal SOCKS endpoint rejects unowned ingress. 5. Swift Testing and fault-injection coverage verifies framing, batching budgets, saturation, MTU errors, cleanup, repeated start and stop, IPv4, and IPv6. 6. Physical evidence on the physical Apple-silicon Mac covers MTUs 1500, 4096, and 8500; the physical-iPhone row is a named deferred gap under ADR-024, never inferred from Mac results and never a pass; constrained buffers; receiver stalls; nominal mixed traffic; staged concurrency; and lifecycle loops with device, OS, revisions, traffic shape, counters, descriptors, CPU, syscall rate, physical footprint, peak, and available-memory samples. 7. A gate report selects measured MTU, buffer, batch, and session baselines, states whether the 25–30 MiB target is met, and permits a HEV fork only when Instruments evidence shows a material bottleneck and a measured callback improvement.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-l2i2oo_target-project-architecture/README.md:10:1. An architecture decision records the chosen generator, exact supported deployment targets, target graph, module ownership, dependency direction, configuration strategy, and reasons against viable alternatives. 2. Clean generation produces buildable ReluxProxyMac, ReluxProxyMacTunnel, ReluxProxyIOS, ReluxProxyIOSTunnel, ReluxTunnelCore, ReluxTunnelHarness, relux-relay, and protocol-test targets with deterministic identifiers and schemes. 3. Both providers are thin adapters that depend on shared core contracts and do not duplicate packet, SSH, relay, lifecycle, or memory state machines. 4. Native Swift and C dependencies can be pinned, licensed, and linked without dynamic-library or App Store extension violations. 5. Credential-free CI or local verification builds every target and runs shared tests; signed physical-device configurations consume Gate P0 identities without committing secrets. 6. The existing SwiftPM tests, manual SOCKS app build, defaults, and release path remain reproducible until an explicit migration decision says otherwise. 7. Developer documentation explains generation, target ownership, signing inputs, artifact locations, and how to run the harness.
EPIC-260715-2mqgvm_viability-and-foundation/STORY-260715-lkshfz_ssh-engine-spike/README.md:10:1. Both candidates build in the macOS and iOS packet-tunnel target configurations, with pinned revisions, licenses, integration constraints, and reproducible build commands recorded. 2. One common conformance harness obtains raw host-key evidence before acceptance and exercises Ed25519 plus an approved fallback against current OpenSSH, documented older profiles, and the real relux host. 3. Candidate evidence covers independent direct-tcpip and bidirectional long-lived exec channels, early close and half-close, bounded backpressure, cancellation, network loss, reconnect, and no task, channel, socket, or descriptor growth across repeated cycles. 4. Initial receive windows and WINDOW_ADJUST behavior are configurable per channel; tests cover 32 KiB, 64 KiB, and capped bandwidth-delay-product profiles without multiplying a large default by maximum sessions. 5. Client byte and time thresholds and server requests survive at least 5 GiB of simultaneous direct-tcpip and exec traffic, with lane-local failure evidence. 6. Hundreds of concurrent channels and one, two, and four connection trials remain within the extension memory budget and record device, OS, revisions, algorithms, traffic, loss and latency, windows, queued bytes, CPU, physical footprint, peak, and failures. 7. A decision record selects only a candidate that passes every required gate, documents rejected results and residual risks, and routes a red primary candidate to the alternate rather than weakening criteria.
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-21c23x_quic-and-route-policies/README.md:10:1. Allow QUIC forwards eligible UDP/443 only through a current validated relay generation, Block UDP/443 returns the documented fast local failure, and neither mode silently times out or affects unrelated UDP or tunnel DNS. 2. Auto uses versioned lane-health inputs, deterministic thresholds and hysteresis, rejects new UDP/443 quickly when QUIC-over-SSH is predicted to underperform, and records only aggregate reasons. 3. Full, degraded, reasserting, failed, stopping, and stale-generation states each have one explicit UDP/443 outcome with no physical socket fallback. 4. Compatible mode installs dual-stack defaults and only approved exact exclusions, while fail-closed mode enables includeAllNetworks only where supported and exposes the documented system exceptions without absolute claims. 5. Startup, live mode change, endpoint replacement, reconnect, settings failure, and stop apply or roll back route and DNS settings without a recursive route, broad exclusion, false-connected snapshot, or ordinary DNS leak. 6. Unit, fault, and golden tests cover policy decisions, fast-failure latency, unrelated traffic, platform branches, exact routes, rollback, and redaction. 7. Physical Apple-silicon Mac captures, with iPhone rows deferred-unavailable under ADR-024, across compatible and fail-closed modes and full, degraded, and reasserting capabilities match the disclosed traffic matrix and record OS behavior changes.
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-2indo6_ssh-lane-pool-and-scheduler/README.md:10:1. One mandatory control lane starts first and optional general or bulk lanes open only after identity, server-limit, and memory admission checks. 2. Every lane proves the same approved host identity and credential generation, and any mismatch closes that lane before channels open. 3. New flows are assigned from documented congestion and policy inputs, remain pinned until close, and Lane D never retroactively adopts an existing flow. 4. Control, relay, and latency-sensitive DNS remain serviceable under general or bulk congestion within recorded latency bounds. 5. Rekey and failed-lane states stop new assignment on the affected lane, preserve valid existing channel state where possible, and route control-lane loss through an explicit degraded or recovery transition without DNS or UDP leakage. 6. Queues, lanes, and channels stay inside configured ceilings and soft memory pressure suppresses optional capacity. 7. Deterministic tests and physical Apple-silicon Mac trials, with iPhone rows deferred-unavailable under ADR-024, at one, two, and four lanes record loss, latency, throughput, queues, windows, channels, memory, CPU, errors, and cleanup and demonstrate measured head-of-line improvement without resource growth.
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-2txwb7_path-reconnect-state-machine/README.md:10:1. A complete transition table defines every state, event, readiness predicate, cleanup action, retry class, capability, and published reason and rejects illegal or stale transitions. 2. Reconnect first tries verified cached endpoint IPs and uses an NWConnection constrained to the selected physical interface for fresh resolution or connect when required, then records the actual remote endpoint. 3. Host-key mismatch, authentication rejection, and invalid configuration are terminal until user or configuration change, while transient path errors use bounded jittered retry that resets only after stable service. 4. The exact endpoint exclusion and network settings are replaced for the current generation before packet reads and capability publication, with no recursive route loop or broad bypass. 5. Mandatory lane A, safe DNS, packet and TCP paths, relay capability, and optional lanes restore in the documented order, and reasserting clears only when the advertised full or degraded mode is usable. 6. User stop promptly cancels paths, timers, attempts, settings work, lanes, tasks, and descriptors, and reconnect overlap obeys the memory reservation. 7. Fault tests and physical Wi-Fi to cellular, cellular to Wi-Fi, loss, and server-address-change trials prove zero ordinary DNS or traffic fallback, bounded outage and retries, generation safety, and resource return to baseline.
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-2txwb7_path-reconnect-state-machine/README.md:4:Implement a generation-safe reconnect controller that reacts to physical-path, SSH, relay, viability, and lifecycle events, reconnects through the selected physical interface, rebuilds the exact endpoint exclusion and tunnel settings, restores safe DNS and capabilities in order, and keeps ordinary traffic scoped to the VPN while reasserting.
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-2txwb7_path-reconnect-state-machine/README.md:7:In scope: explicit disconnected, connecting, connected-full, connected-degraded, reasserting, failed, and disconnecting states; NWPath and viability inputs; cached endpoint attempts; required-interface resolution and connect; actual IPv4, IPv6, or synthesized endpoint capture; verified host identity continuity; bounded jittered retry; terminal authentication and host-key behavior; generation and stale-callback rejection; old/new transport memory reservation; exact host-route exclusion replacement; atomic network settings; mandatory control lane, packet plane, TCP, safe DNS, relay, and optional-lane restoration order; provider reasserting and capability snapshots; cancellation, diagnostics, deterministic faults, and physical path-change evidence. Out of scope: silently accepting host changes, broad subnet exclusions, physical DNS fallback, preserving UDP associations across transport generations, final NAT64, sleep, or captive matrices owned by STORY-260715-3ao1u9, final route-mode implementation owned by STORY-260715-21c23x, or UI presentation.
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-3ao1u9_nat64-sleep-and-captive-validation/README.md:10:1. A versioned device, OS, interface, address-family, route-mode, event, traffic, and capture matrix names every required row, fixture, metric, expected outcome, and evidence location before physical execution. 2. IPv6-only and NAT64 starts and transitions use the actual connected endpoint and an exact current-generation exclusion, preserve IPv4 and IPv6 application behavior where supported, and never deadlock in stale routes. 3. Sleep and wake produce at most one current reconnect sequence, cancel stale work, restore only truthful capability, and keep reconnect overlap within the memory ceiling. 4. Captive-network handling matches compatible and fail-closed disclosures and Apple exceptions while ordinary traffic and DNS remain tunneled or fail explicitly. 5. Containing-app termination does not tear down a healthy provider, while cancellation, provider stop, and repeated lifecycle loops release routes, tasks, timers, channels, associations, sockets, and descriptors. 6. Deterministic fault tests cover synthesized-address changes, sleep races, captive transitions, UI termination, stop during start, late callbacks, and API capability changes. 7. Physical Apple-silicon Mac evidence records every supported row, keeps iPhone rows as explicit deferred-unavailable entries under ADR-024, identifies red or unavailable rows without waiver, and publishes regression triggers when OS behavior differs.
EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-3ao1u9_nat64-sleep-and-captive-validation/README.md:4:Harden and validate the M3 runtime across IPv6-only NAT64, address-family changes, sleep and wake, captive-network transitions, containing-app termination, provider stops, and repeated lifecycle loops on the named physical Apple-silicon Mac configuration; the physical-iPhone configurations are deferred with iOS under ADR-024 as named gaps that are never inferred from Mac results.
EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/README.md:10:One configured SSH profile provides stable full-device TCP and leak-free DNS on the physical Apple-silicon Mac; the physical-iPhone row is a named deferred gap under ADR-024, never inferred from Mac results and never a pass; external IP follows the exit host; route loops and UI-process dependency are absent; validation evidence is attached.
EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-2bfjhn_dns-and-ip-routing/README.md:10:1. The actual SSH endpoint is captured before default routes and only the required endpoint host exclusion is derived. 2. IPv4 and IPv6 application TCP settings use compatible mode without routing SSH into itself. 3. Installed DNS points only to the tunnel service and all client UDP or TCP queries use the approved SSH exit resolver policy with bounded protocol semantics. 4. Resolver or tunnel failure never produces a physical DNS query and instead fails explicitly or stops safe forwarding. 5. Deterministic captures prove external-IP, route, and no-fallback behavior, and the physical Apple-silicon Mac acceptance is executed by TASK-260715-2wqffe; TASK-260715-2qr5aj carries the iPhone row and is deferred with iOS under ADR-024.
EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-2wjwuf_ssh-profile-auth-and-host-verification/README.md:10:1. Invalid or stale profiles and inaccessible credential references fail before routes or packet forwarding. 2. Raw host-key evidence is evaluated before user authentication, approved fingerprints connect, first use returns trust-required, and changed keys fail without retry or destination channels. 3. Private keys and passphrases remain in the Data Protection Keychain access group and never enter App Group data, providerConfiguration, logs, diagnostics, or board artifacts. 4. The selected M0 adapter authenticates Ed25519 and the approved fallback against controlled fixtures while cancellation and failure paths return resources to baseline. 5. The session bootstrap returns the verified identity and actual IPv4 or IPv6 endpoint through a provider-ready boundary, with final physical Apple-silicon Mac verification delegated to TASK-260715-3f4rhy and the iPhone row deferred under ADR-024.
EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-eto58m_vpn-manager-and-extension-lifecycle/README.md:10:1. Each platform creates or updates only the Relux-owned manager configuration and does not duplicate or overwrite unrelated VPN configurations. 2. The thin providers delegate live work to ReluxTunnelCore, report versioned state, and stop promptly with all owned work cancelled. 3. Forwarding and truthful system VPN state continue while the containing app is suspended or terminated. 4. Automated lifecycle tests cover persistence, start cancellation, provider failures, messages, stop reasons, stale configuration, and repeated cycles. 5. Physical Apple-silicon Mac evidence proves, with the iPhone row deferred under ADR-024, approved host verification and auth, dual-stack representative TCP, leak-free DNS, exit-host external IP, no route loop, host-app independence, disconnect, and cleanup.
EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-eto58m_vpn-manager-and-extension-lifecycle/README.md:4:Implement the macOS host and provider lifecycle — the iOS adapter stays defined but deferred under ADR-024 and re-arms unchanged — needed to install, start, observe, and stop one owned custom VPN configuration while keeping the packet-tunnel extension authoritative. Integrate the completed SSH, TCP, DNS, and routing core in the real providers and own the physical Apple-silicon Mac acceptance evidence, with the physical-iPhone rows deferred with iOS under ADR-024 as named gaps.
EPIC-260715-3810we_tcp-dns-system-vpn/STORY-260715-eto58m_vpn-manager-and-extension-lifecycle/README.md:7:In scope: idempotent NETunnelProviderManager ownership, save and reload, permission integration, NETunnelProviderSession control, thin iOS and macOS provider adapters, versioned app messaging, truthful status, stop and cleanup, host suspension or termination independence, provider-context SSH authentication, dual-stack TCP and DNS routing, authorized leak evidence, repeated lifecycle tests, and physical-device runbooks. Out of scope: profile editor UX, final visual design, UDP relay, path reconnect, fail-closed routing, TestFlight, notarization, and repeating M0 entitlement or engine gates.
EPIC-260715-w5gzf4_release-and-distribution/STORY-260715-2dtdql_release-operations-privacy-and-review/README.md:10:1. A versioned release manifest and runbook identify candidate commits and artifacts, owners, approvals, required CI gates, promotion order, communication, retention, abort criteria, rollback, revocation, and post-release checks for relay, macOS, and iOS. 2. A currently dated primary-source review plus accountable product, privacy, legal, security, and regional approvals produce public policy, App Privacy labels, export-compliance answers, retention or deletion commitments, and storefront availability that match actual behavior. 3. The App Review package accurately explains the VPN data path, Network Extension purpose, user-owned SSH exit, observer boundaries, system exclusions, full and degraded modes, diagnostics, support data, and regional limits without claiming anonymity or a Relux-operated exit service. 4. Reviewers receive a reproducible test profile or isolated SSH host and instructions that exercise the submitted binary without exposing private production credentials; access is scoped, monitored for abuse, rotated, and removed after review. 5. The exact signed candidates pass the supported simulator, physical iPhone, Apple-silicon Mac, accessibility, privacy, leak, lifecycle, entitlement, notarization, TestFlight, artifact, migration, install, upgrade, and rollback matrix, with failures blocking promotion. 6. Submission metadata, screenshots, privacy and support URLs, binary and build identifiers, reviewer notes, communications, rejections, corrections, and resubmissions are preserved and traceable to the reviewed behavior and evidence revision.
EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2byjks_m5-release-gates-and-ceremonies/README.md:4:M5 human gates (macOS-first: every TestFlight and App Review item below is deferred with iOS and the App Store branch under ADR-013/ADR-024, stays defined, and re-arms unchanged): Apple portal certificate/environment provisioning, TestFlight physical lifecycle runs, clean-Mac install/uninstall with system-extension GUI approval, distribution acceptance, privacy/legal declarations and regional VPN licensing decisions, privacy policy legal approval, App Review submission package human dry-run, submission and feedback triage, go/no-go decisions, rollback and credential-revocation ceremonies, post-publication channel watch.
EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2cjf4i_m2-physical-validation/README.md:4:Physical Apple-silicon Mac validation of M2 UDP relay and degraded mode, with the physical-iPhone rows deferred with iOS under ADR-024: UDP/DNS exit runs and full/degraded/recovery transitions on real devices.
EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2e86nb_m3-physical-validation/README.md:10:1. Every physical task uses the accepted TASK-260715-2kchi0 protocol and validator, names device/OS/toolchain/revisions/configuration/fixtures, and publishes content-addressed privacy-safe raw references plus pass, red, unavailable or invalid status for every required row. 2. The macOS-first baseline and supported Mac lifecycle/route rows execute without waiting for iPhone; iPhone rows remain explicit deferred-unavailable entries until authorized hardware and provisioning exist and are never inferred from Mac results. 3. Byte and protocol integrity, zero ordinary DNS fallback, no recursive or broad bypass routes, bounded queues and memory, truthful failure/capability behavior, nominal no-drop behavior and exact cleanup are non-tradeable gates across every matrix. 4. Any accepted tuning value has paired untuned before-and-after evidence meeting the fixed statistical and practical-effect rules, records rejected candidates and device/OS variance, and leaves all red rows visible. 5. The final locked matrix covers every supported packet, lane, rekey, pressure, reconnect, family, sleep, captive, QUIC and route-mode row or records a concrete unavailable/red owner, accepted regression baseline, policy ratification and reproducible evidence manifest.
EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2e86nb_m3-physical-validation/README.md:4:Execute the TASK-260715-2kchi0 evidence protocol on named physical Macs and, when authorized hardware is available, schema-compatible iPhones to establish untuned baselines, exercise lane, rekey, memory, reconnect, NAT64, lifecycle, QUIC and route safety, tune only from accepted comparisons, and publish a locked final M3 matrix with explicit red and unavailable rows.
EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2m2tl1_m0-viability-manual-gates/README.md:4:Human gates for M0 viability: Apple DTS/authoritative evidence, Gate A0/P0 dispositions, physical Apple-silicon Mac packet-tunnel probe verification, with the physical-iPhone probe deferred with iOS under ADR-024, Apple Developer portal provisioning and account readiness, physical memory/MTU/SSH-comparative matrices feeding the M0 engine and bridge decisions.
EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-d37sts_m1-physical-validation/README.md:4:Physical Apple-silicon Mac validation of the M1 TCP+DNS system VPN, with the physical-iPhone rows deferred with iOS under ADR-024: provider lifecycle and UI-independence, routing and DNS leak evidence, in-provider SSH authentication. Requires device hands and system VPN/extension GUI approvals.
EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260819-r1cx8r_dedicated-macos-vpn-test-host-safety/README.md:7:macOS physical VPN validation only; the current build host remains build/unit/integration-test only.
